                <h1>TikTok Shop 新品冷启动素材池搭建 SOP</h1>
<p>新品上架后，TikTok Shop 素材通常面临两个问题：素材量少、模型学习慢；素材多了，又容易出现重复内容和投放异常。本手册按执行顺序搭建素材池，适合广告、内容和店铺运营共同协作。</p>
<h2>1. 先定义测试目标，不要直接复制旧素材</h2>
<h3>第一步：明确单品阶段</h3>
<p>将新品分为三类：</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>验证型</strong>：卖点、使用场景或价格尚未确认，需要测试受众与内容方向。</li>
<li><strong>成长型</strong>：已有初步点击和加购数据，需要扩大素材覆盖。</li>
<li><strong>放量型</strong>：订单与退款趋于稳定，可增加达人素材和直播切片。</li>
</ul>
<h3>第二步：建立统一命名规则</h3>
<p>建议格式：</p>
<p><code>产品_卖点_场景_形式_版本_日期</code></p>
<p>例如：<code>清洁刷_省时_浴室_达人实拍_V1_0318</code>。</p>
<p>同一文件不要重复上传。素材命名应与广告组、落地页版本保持一致，方便后续对账和判断素材失效原因。</p>
<h2>2. 用“3×3”结构搭出首批素材池</h2>
<p>每个卖点准备三个表达角度，每个角度制作两种内容形式，形成至少 9 组基础素材：</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>内容角度</th>
<th>形式 A</th>
<th>形式 B</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>痛点场景</td>
<td>问题前后对比</td>
<td>口播说明</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>核心卖点</td>
<td>细节特写</td>
<td>字幕卡点</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>使用结果</td>
<td>真人体验</td>
<td>达人口播</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>素材需在前 2—3 秒展示商品或明确场景，避免开场铺垫过长。字幕突出一个核心信息，同时保留价格、优惠条件和适用人群等信息。若商品存在特殊限制，应在画面和落地页中同步说明。</p>
<h2>3. 建立脚本模板库，降低内容生产成本</h2>
<p>脚本按“钩子—问题—证据—行动”编写：</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>钩子</strong>：直接呈现问题或结果。</li>
<li><strong>问题</strong>：说明用户为什么需要该商品。</li>
<li><strong>证据</strong>：通过实拍、细节或使用过程展示卖点。</li>
<li><strong>行动</strong>：引导查看商品、领取优惠或完成购买。</li>
</ol>
<p>不要把模板理解为固定文案。同一模板可替换开场、人物、场景和卖点顺序。建议为每个卖点保留 3 个钩子、2 个证明方式、2 个行动引导，组合后生成不同视频。</p>
<h2>4. 首轮测试：小预算并行，减少变量</h2>
<p>首轮测试应让不同卖点同时获得曝光机会，而不是把预算集中在单条素材上。</p>
<h3>执行动作</h3>
<ul>
<li>按卖点拆分广告组，避免不同受众、预算和素材混在同一组。</li>
<li>使用相近出价与投放目标，尽量只比较素材差异。</li>
<li>设置每日消耗上限，并提前配置账户余额预警。</li>
<li>优先观察展示、点击、视频停留、商品点击和加购，不急于仅凭点击率决定去留。</li>
<li>对高点击但低加购的素材，检查落地页价格、物流、优惠信息和商品页一致性。</li>
</ul>
<p>若素材消耗达到账户常规测试门槛仍无有效信号，可进入淘汰池；具体门槛应结合客单价、历史转化周期和平台建议设置。</p>
<h2>5. 每日迭代：保留、衍生还是淘汰</h2>
<p>将素材分为三个处理组：</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>保留</strong>：产生稳定商品点击、订单或有效加购。</li>
<li><strong>衍生</strong>：钩子有效，但中途流失明显，可替换卖点顺序、缩短开场或补充字幕。</li>
<li><strong>淘汰</strong>：持续消耗但无有效反馈，或出现重复、违规、加载异常。</li>
</ul>
<p>每次只修改一个主要变量，并记录改动内容。不要仅因单日波动频繁调整预算。新素材应从高表现视频中衍生，保留原商品场景和核心卖点，再测试新钩子、新人物或新节奏。</p>
<h2>6. 放量前完成四项检查</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>内容合规</strong>：核对平台政策、商品资质、达人授权和促销表述。</li>
<li><strong>素材去重</strong>：检查音频、画面、字幕和脚本是否高度相似。</li>
<li><strong>链路一致</strong>：视频展示的价格、优惠和商品页信息必须一致。</li>
<li><strong>账户与资金安全</strong>：关注异常消耗、拒付、支付风险及余额变化；充值前核对币种、账户主体和到账状态。</li>
</ol>
<p>完成检查后，可逐步提高预算，不要一次性大幅修改预算、出价和素材。保留每轮测试记录，并将素材消耗、订单、退款与广告账户支出定期对账。</p>
<h2>7. 素材池交付清单</h2>
<ul>
<li>首批至少覆盖 3 个卖点、3 类场景和多种拍摄形式。</li>
<li>每个素材都有统一命名、版本号和测试目标。</li>
<li>脚本、原始视频、成片、字幕与封面均已归档。</li>
<li>明确保留、衍生和淘汰标签。</li>
<li>预算上限、余额预警与异常消耗处理责任人已确认。</li>
<li>投放数据可按素材、达人、卖点及日期追踪。</li>
</ul>
<p>冷启动的核心不是一次做出“爆款”，而是快速建立可验证、可复用、可持续迭代的素材系统。</p>
            </div>
            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h1>TikTok Shop New Product Cold-Start Creative Pool SOP</h1>
<p>After a new product goes live, TikTok Shop campaigns often face two challenges: too few creatives and slow algorithmic learning. Uploading too many assets can create duplicate-content and account-compliance risks. This SOP provides a step-by-end workflow for advertising, content, and storefront teams.</p>
<h2>1. Define the Testing Objective Before Reusing Old Creatives</h2>
<h3>Step 1: Classify the product stage</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Validation stage</strong>: The target audience, key selling point, or price positioning is unclear.</li>
<li><strong>Growth stage</strong>: Initial clicks and add-to-cart actions have been observed, and broader creative coverage is needed.</li>
<li><strong>Scaling stage</strong>: Orders and refunds are relatively stable, allowing the team to expand creator content and livestream clips.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Step 2: Adopt a consistent naming convention</h3>
<p>Use:</p>
<p><code>Product_SellingPoint_Scenario_Format_Version_Date</code></p>
<p>For example: <code>CleaningBrush_TimeSaving_Bathroom_CreatorDemo_V1_0318</code>.</p>
<p>Avoid uploading the same file multiple times. Align creative names with ad group and landing-page versions so the team can identify the reason behind performance changes.</p>
<h2>2. Build the First Asset Pool with a 3×3 Framework</h2>
<p>For each selling point, prepare three communication angles and two creative formats, producing at least nine test combinations:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Communication angle</th>
<th>Format A</th>
<th>Format B</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Pain-point scenario</td>
<td>Before-and-after demonstration</td>
<td>Voice-over explanation</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Core benefit</td>
<td>Detail close-up</td>
<td>Caption-led video</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Usage result</td>
<td>Real-user test</td>
<td>Creator review</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Show the product or a clear use case within the first two to three seconds. Avoid lengthy introductions. Each video should emphasize one primary message while communicating relevant pricing, promotions, and product conditions. If the product has usage restrictions, disclose them both in the video and on the product page.</p>
<h2>3. Create a Script Template Library</h2>
<p>Structure every script around four elements:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Hook</strong>: Present the problem or result immediately.</li>
<li><strong>Problem</strong>: Explain why the customer needs the product.</li>
<li><strong>Proof</strong>: Demonstrate the benefit through footage, details, or real-world use.</li>
<li><strong>Call to action</strong>: Direct viewers to the product page, promotion, or checkout.</li>
</ol>
<p>Templates should not become rigid scripts. Replace the opening, presenter, setting, and sequence of selling points. For each benefit, keep three hooks, two proof methods, and two calls to action so the team can generate varied assets efficiently.</p>
<h2>4. Run the Initial Test with Controlled Variables</h2>
<p>Give each selling point a fair opportunity to receive impressions instead of concentrating the budget on one asset.</p>
<h3>Actions</h3>
<ul>
<li>Separate ad groups by selling point and avoid mixing audiences, budgets, and creatives in one group.</li>
<li>Keep bids and optimization goals similar so the test focuses primarily on creative performance.</li>
<li>Set daily spending limits and configure account balance alerts before launch.</li>
<li>Review impressions, clicks, watch time, product clicks, and add-to-cart actions rather than making decisions based on click-through rate alone.</li>
<li>If a creative attracts clicks but generates few add-to-cart actions, check price, shipping information, promotions, and consistency between the video and product page.</li>
</ul>
<p>Creatives that spend beyond the account’s normal testing threshold without producing meaningful signals can be moved to the rejection pool. The threshold should reflect the product’s average order value, historical conversion window, and platform recommendations.</p>
<h2>5. Iterate Daily: Keep, Derive, or Reject</h2>
<p>Manage assets in three groups:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Keep</strong>: Creatives that consistently generate product clicks, orders, or meaningful add-to-cart actions.</li>
<li><strong>Derive</strong>: Hooks that work but lose viewers midway. Change the selling-point order, shorten the opening, or improve captions.</li>
<li><strong>Reject</strong>: Assets that continue spending without meaningful feedback, or videos affected by duplication, policy, and loading issues.</li>
</ul>
<p>Change only one major variable per iteration and document each modification. Avoid frequent budget changes based on a single day’s fluctuations. Create new assets from strong videos while retaining the product context and core selling point, then test a new hook, presenter, or editing rhythm.</p>
<h2>6. Complete Four Checks Before Scaling</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Content compliance</strong>: Review platform policies, product qualifications, creator permissions, and promotional claims.</li>
<li><strong>Creative deduplication</strong>: Check whether audio, visuals, captions, and scripts are highly similar.</li>
<li><strong>Journey consistency</strong>: Prices, promotions, and product information shown in the video must match the product page.</li>
<li><strong>Account and funding security</strong>: Monitor abnormal spending, chargebacks, payment risks, and balance changes. Before recharging, verify the currency, account entity, and credited amount.</li>
</ol>
<p>Increase budgets gradually after these checks. Do not change the budget, bid, and creative assets simultaneously at a large scale. Keep records of every test cycle and reconcile creative spend, orders, refunds, and advertising-account expenses regularly.</p>
<h2>7. Creative Pool Delivery Checklist</h2>
<ul>
<li>The initial batch covers at least three selling points, three scenarios, and multiple production formats.</li>
<li>Every asset has a consistent name, version, and testing objective.</li>
<li>Scripts, raw footage, final edits, captions, and covers are archived.</li>
<li>Keep, derive, and reject labels are assigned.</li>
<li>Spending limits, balance alerts, and the owner for handling abnormal consumption are confirmed.</li>
<li>Performance can be tracked by creative, creator, selling point, and date.</li>
</ul>
<p>The objective of cold start is not to create an instant viral hit. It is to build a creative system that can be tested, reused, and improved quickly.</p>
